CAT = "phnleChn"; SEG = "CHN"
# Nursing Practice I: community health nursing, family health, epidemiology, DOH programmes and
# communicable disease control in the Philippine setting. 28 items, 7 per test.
Q = [
("The Rural Health Unit nurse in a municipality in Samar wants the barangay to own its new water project. Which step of community organizing comes first?",
 "Entry and integration", ["Community mobilization", "Phase-out and turnover", "Core group formation"],
 "In COPAR the worker enters and integrates with the people before forming a core group or mobilizing. Phase-out is the last step, when the community runs the project itself."),
("In the typology of family nursing problems, a mother who smokes inside a house where an infant sleeps is an example of a:",
 "Health threat", ["Health deficit", "Foreseeable crisis", "Wellness state"],
 "A health threat is a condition that favors disease or injury, like passive smoke exposure. A health deficit is an illness or disability already present."),
("A family's recent move to a new city, expected to strain the household, is classified in the typology of nursing problems as a:",
 "Foreseeable crisis", ["Health threat", "Health deficit", "Wellness potential"],
 "Anticipated periods of unusual demand, such as relocation, school entry or retirement, are foreseeable crises or stress points."),
("When ranking family health problems, which criterion reflects how serious and urgent the family itself thinks the problem is?",
 "Salience", ["Modifiability of the problem", "Preventive potential", "Nature of the problem"],
 "In Bailon and Maglaya's four criteria, salience is the family's own perception of the problem's seriousness and urgency. Modifiability is the chance that intervention succeeds; preventive potential is how much future harm intervention can prevent."),
("A barangay of 4,000 people recorded 96 live births last year. What is its crude birth rate?",
 "24 per 1,000", ["2.4 per 1,000", "96 per 1,000", "4.2 per 1,000"],
 "Crude birth rate = live births / midyear population x 1,000 = 96 / 4,000 x 1,000 = 24. 2.4 forgets that the rate is per 1,000, not per 100."),
("The maternal mortality ratio is expressed per how many live births?",
 "100,000", ["1,000", "10,000", "1,000,000"],
 "Maternal deaths are rare, so the ratio is per 100,000 live births. Infant and neonatal mortality rates use 1,000 live births."),
("Of 60 guests at a fiesta lunch, 15 developed vomiting within 6 hours. What is the attack rate?",
 "25%", ["15%", "4%", "40%"],
 "Attack rate = new cases / population at risk x 100 = 15 / 60 x 100 = 25%. It is the incidence rate used in outbreaks."),
("Teaching a group of farmers to wear boots in flooded rice fields during the rainy season is which level of prevention?",
 "Primary", ["Secondary", "Tertiary", "Quaternary"],
 "Boots prevent leptospirosis before infection happens, which is primary prevention. Early diagnosis is secondary; rehabilitation is tertiary."),
("Within how many hours of birth should a newborn receive the first dose of hepatitis B vaccine?",
 "24 hours", ["48 hours", "72 hours", "7 days"],
 "The birth dose of hepatitis B vaccine is given within 24 hours to prevent mother-to-child transmission. BCG is also given at birth."),
("In the RHU cold chain, which vaccine may be kept frozen because freezing does not damage it?",
 "Oral polio vaccine", ["Hepatitis B vaccine", "Pentavalent vaccine", "Tetanus-diphtheria vaccine"],
 "OPV tolerates freezing and is the most heat-sensitive vaccine. Vaccines with an aluminum adjuvant, such as hepatitis B, pentavalent and Td, are ruined by freezing."),
("Under the Expanded Newborn Screening law (R.A. 9288), the blood sample is collected:",
 "After 24 hours, within 3 days", ["Within the first hour of life", "On the seventh day of life", "At the 6-week immunization visit"],
 "R.A. 9288 sets screening after 24 hours of life but not later than three days from complete delivery; an infant in intensive care may be screened by seven days."),
("A mother refuses newborn screening for her baby because of her religious beliefs. What should the nurse do?",
 "Document a signed refusal", ["Collect the sample anyway", "Report her to the barangay", "Delay discharge until she agrees"],
 "R.A. 9288 lets a parent refuse on religious grounds; the refusal is acknowledged in writing and kept in the newborn's record."),
("A dog that bit a child in Batangas looks healthy. How long should it be observed for signs of rabies?",
 "14 days", ["3 days", "30 days", "6 months"],
 "The dog is observed for 14 days. If it stays healthy, it was not shedding rabies virus at the time of the bite; post-exposure care still starts at once."),
("What is the first-aid step for a dog bite wound before the patient reaches the animal bite center?",
 "Wash with soap and water", ["Apply a tight tourniquet", "Suture the wound closed", "Cover with an herbal poultice"],
 "Washing the wound with soap and running water for several minutes removes much of the virus. Bite wounds are generally not sutured right away."),
("The schistosomiasis parasite found in Leyte, Samar and Mindanao is carried by which intermediate host?",
 "Freshwater snail", ["Aedes mosquito", "Field rat", "Freshwater carp"],
 "Schistosoma japonicum develops in the Oncomelania snail; people are infected when cercariae pierce the skin in snail-infested water."),
("Which mosquito transmits dengue in Philippine households?",
 "Aedes aegypti", ["Anopheles flavirostris", "Culex quinquefasciatus", "Aedes poicilius"],
 "Aedes aegypti breeds in clean stored water and bites by day. Anopheles flavirostris carries malaria; Aedes poicilius carries filariasis in some provinces."),
("During a dengue outbreak, the barangay health worker leads a search-and-destroy campaign. Which action fits it?",
 "Empty water in old tires", ["Spray indoors at midnight", "Give antibiotics to contacts", "Isolate fever cases at home"],
 "Search and destroy removes breeding sites: clean, still water in tires, cans, vases and drums. Aedes bites by day, so night spraying misses it."),
("A patient on the TB DOTS program asks why the health worker must watch him swallow every dose. The nurse explains it mainly:",
 "Ensures complete treatment", ["Prevents drug allergy", "Speeds sputum conversion", "Lowers the drug cost"],
 "Directly observed treatment makes sure every dose is taken, which cures the patient and prevents drug-resistant TB."),
("Mass drug administration in endemic provinces is the main strategy against which disease?",
 "Lymphatic filariasis", ["Dengue fever", "Leptospirosis", "Schistosomiasis"],
 "The filariasis elimination program gives yearly mass drug administration to everyone eligible in endemic areas to interrupt transmission."),
("A child aged 3 years attends Garantisadong Pambata. What vitamin A dose does the child receive?",
 "200,000 IU", ["100,000 IU", "50,000 IU", "10,000 IU"],
 "Children 12 to 59 months receive 200,000 IU of vitamin A; infants 6 to 11 months receive 100,000 IU."),
("Which law created the Universal Health Care program that enrolls every Filipino in PhilHealth?",
 "R.A. 11223", ["R.A. 10354", "R.A. 11036", "R.A. 9288"],
 "R.A. 11223 (2019) is the Universal Health Care Act. R.A. 10354 is the RPRH law, R.A. 11036 the Mental Health Act and R.A. 9288 newborn screening."),
("A household gets water from a public faucet that serves several homes from a piped system. This is which level of water supply?",
 "Level II", ["Level I", "Level III", "Level IV"],
 "Level II is a communal faucet system. Level I is a point source such as a protected well; Level III has faucets in each household."),
("A baby is born at home with a hilot and brought to the RHU the next day. Under R.A. 10152, the hepatitis B birth dose for such an infant must be given within 24 hours and no later than:",
 "7 days after birth", ["14 days after birth", "6 weeks after birth", "1 month after birth"],
 "R.A. 10152 (2011) requires the hepatitis B vaccine within 24 hours of birth; an infant delivered without a health professional should be brought to a facility within 24 hours, and no later than seven days."),
("Residents of three barangays along a river organize because they share a common concern about flooding. They form a:",
 "Functional community", ["Geographic community", "Political community", "Virtual community"],
 "A functional community is defined by a shared interest or problem, not by boundaries. A geographic community shares a place."),
("Which home visit principle should guide the frequency of a public health nurse's visits?",
 "The family's needs", ["The nurse's free days", "The family's income", "The barangay's size"],
 "Visit frequency depends on the family's health needs and the resources available, with priority to those at greatest risk."),
("In the public health bag technique, where should the nurse place the bag in the client's home?",
 "On a clean, flat surface", ["On the floor near the door", "On the patient's bed", "Inside a kitchen cabinet"],
 "The bag goes on a clean, flat surface lined with paper, away from children and pets, and is opened only after hand washing."),
("A case of acute flaccid paralysis in a 9-year-old is reported at once to the surveillance unit. Which disease is being watched for?",
 "Poliomyelitis", ["Neonatal tetanus", "Meningitis", "Diphtheria"],
 "Every case of acute flaccid paralysis in a child under 15 is investigated to detect polio, which the Philippines has eliminated and keeps under surveillance."),
("Which is the most useful single sign the nurse checks to detect a child with pneumonia during an IMCI assessment?",
 "Fast breathing", ["Runny nose", "Cough for two days", "Mild fever"],
 "IMCI classifies pneumonia by fast breathing or chest indrawing. Cough and runny nose alone point to a cold."),
]
